gns; e.g. the bottom‑band unit 8 pushes strongly *against* class 9 (−1.8) and class 7 (−0.9) and *for* classes 6 and 1; the bottom‑band unit 10 pushes for classes 7 and 3 and against 4. Biases favour classes 1, 2, 5, 7 and penalize 8 and 0.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\n- MNIST (and KMNIST, EMNIST) digits/characters are size‑normalized into a 20×20 box and centred, so rows 0–3 and 24–27 are essentially always black and their W1 rows would stay at tiny initialization values. Here rows 0–3 and 23–25 carry some of the strongest weights, so those datasets are ruled out.\n- The objects fill the frame to within about a pixel, are wide at the top and bottom, and the useful features are large smooth regions: a solid horizontal mass along the bottom (soles of sneakers/sandals/boots, hems of dresses/coats), broad mass in the upper half (shoulders of shirts/pullovers/coats, tops of bags), a dark/bright narrow vertical strip at the centre (gap between trouser legs vs. solid body of a dress/coat), and an oblique edge in the middle (the slanted shaft of boots / tilted shoes). This is exactly the kind of silhouette information a tiny MLP learns on clothing images.\n- The output structure fits too: a \"bright bottom band\" feature arguing against class 9 and 7 vs for 1 and 6, a trouser‑gap feature, etc., matches Fashion‑MNIST's class semantics (footwear classes 5/7/9 share the bottom, trouser 1 vs dress 3 differ in the central strip, 0/2/4/6 are confusable tops).\n\n**Dataset**: Fashion‑MNIST (Zalando 28×28 grayscale clothing items).\n\n**Classes**: 0 T‑shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence**: 75%",
